# +String, +Words, -Starts
# +String, +Words, -Starts
# ?Point, ?Width, ?Words, ?Distinct, ?Seed, ?Kind
#   * <module> The substrings that concatenate all the words
#
# THE PROBLEM (LeetCode 30, "Substring with Concatenation of All Words"): a
# bit string and a list of bit words, all of one width, repeats allowed; the
# positions at which the string continues with the words, each used once,
# in some order. S(P) is the set of those positions.
#
# The program writes top (corpora/library/top) where the pseudo-code and the
# argument below write {}, the empty choice of the calculus that still has
# it; doc/proofs/reduction_series_answers.md carries the argument over to
# Definition 14, and the generator is the family's Scala object in
# gezi/contest-corpora.
#
# THE PROGRAM, in continuation passing style, with the prelude's bits_prefix,
# length, concat, natural_at_least and successor (the API of
# round-37-directions.md):
#
#     pick   = Y (\self. \ws. \f. ws {} (\w. \rest.
#                  { f w rest, self rest (\v. \others. f v (cons w others)) }))
#     drop   = Y (\self. \n. \s. n s (\m. s nil (\_. \t. self m t)))
#     fit    = Y (\self. \ws. \s. \i. ws i (\_. \_.
#                  pick ws (\w. \rest. bits_prefix w s (self rest (drop (length w) s) i) {})))
#     starts = Y (\self. \s. \i. { at_least (length s) total (fit Words s i) {},
#                                  s {} (\_. \t. self t (successor i)) })
#     program = (\total. starts String 0) (length (concat Words))
#
# pick w rest takes one word out of the list and keeps the others in their
# order, so taking either of two equal words leaves the same list, spelled
# two ways.
# Directions: E (the words matched against the string bit by bit, at every
# position); B (merging by value: the orders in which the equal words are
# taken are exponentially many, the states are a position and the list of
# the words left); D (length of concat of the words); G (the positions); and
# the regression direction 2 of that list (the same list
# of the words left reached through different spellings, cons w others
# rebuilt by pick's continuation).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT. S(P) is the set of the unary numerals of the positions i
# such that the string from i begins with the words concatenated in some
# order, each once: concatenation_starts/3 on small instances, and
# window_starts/3, which cuts the string from each i into as many pieces of
# the width as there are words and compares them with the words as
# multisets (the words having one width, the two agree).
#
# THE LEMMAS, on the definitions of the library functions (contest_attractors
# attractor_source/2 and the prelude), not on their native references.
# ⌜n⌝ is the Scott unary numeral, ⌜0⌝ = λz.λs. z and ⌜n + 1⌝ = λz.λs. s ⌜n⌝,
# which unary/2 builds exactly, successor n one β step from λz.λs. s n;
# true = λt.λf. t and false = λt.λf. f; a list is a Scott list, cons h t
# one β step from λn.λc. c h t; a string or word is a list of Booleans, true
# for 1 and false for 0. Every word is nonempty.
# - (Y) fixpoint G =β G (fixpoint G), two β steps.
# - (Prefix) bits_prefix = Y (λs.λa.λb. a true (λh.λt. b false
#   (λo.λr. h (o (s t r) false) (o false (s t r))))). By induction on a,
#   bits_prefix a b =β true when a is a prefix of b and false otherwise.
# - (Length) length xs =β ⌜|xs|⌝, by induction on xs (length = Y (λs.λxs.
#   xs ⌜0⌝ (λ_.λt. successor (s t)))).
# - (Concat) concat = foldr append nil; append a b =β a followed by b, by
#   induction on a; concat [c1, ..., cj] =β c1 followed by ... cj, by
#   induction on j.
# - (AtLeast) at_least ⌜m⌝ ⌜n⌝ =β true when m >= n and false otherwise, by
#   induction on n (at_least = Y (λs.λm.λn. n true (λn1. m false
#   (λm1. s m1 n1)))).
# - (Drop) drop ⌜n⌝ u =β u without its first n elements, for n =< |u|, by
#   induction on n.
# - (Pick) pick [w1, ..., wm] f =β {f w1 [w2, ..., wm], {f w2 [w1, w3, ...,
#   wm], ... {f wm [w1, ..., wm-1], {}}}}: by induction on m, the second
#   member of each level picks from the rest and puts the word it passed
#   over back in front.
# - (Empty) If U →* {} by β and η steps, no pure term is reached from U by
#   →c steps. Every →c step keeps U →* {}: a β or η step by confluence, {}
#   being normal; a member replacement of a set s by Lemma 2a of
#   certificate_meta_lemmas.md, which replays U →* {} to {} with the
#   descendants of s replaced, and {} is no descendant of s, s having a
#   member and descendants keeping the number of members. A pure term
#   reduces only to pure terms, and {} is not pure.
#
# So fit ws s i, with ws =β a list of words, s =β a string v and i =β ⌜j⌝,
# is β-equal to ⌜j⌝ when ws is empty, and otherwise to the nest of Pick
# whose member for the word w, the rest r, is β-equal to
# fit r (v without its first |w| elements) ⌜j⌝ when w is a prefix of v and
# to {} otherwise (Prefix, Length, Drop). And starts s ⌜j⌝, s =β the suffix
# of the string from j, is β-equal to a set of two members: fit Words s ⌜j⌝
# when the suffix is at least as long as the words together (Length,
# Concat, AtLeast) and {} otherwise; and {} when the suffix is empty,
# starts (the suffix from j + 1) ⌜j + 1⌝ otherwise.
#
# PROOF, with ⟦·⟧, D, S and Th and Lemma 2b of
# gezi/doc/proofs/certificate_meta_lemmas.md.
# - (⊇) For a position j at which the string continues with the words in
#   the order w_π1, ..., w_πm: take the second member of starts j times,
#   then the first; the suffix is long enough. In fit, take at each level
#   the member of Pick for the next word of that order (Lemma 2b.1 on the
#   β-equal term); its prefix test holds. When the words are used up the
#   term is β-equal to ⌜j⌝, pure and normal: it is in that term's meaning
#   (equal terms, Lemma 2b.2) and so in ⟦P⟧ (Lemma 2b.2).
# - (⊆) By Lemma 2b.3 every yield equals a pure term P reaches by a finite
#   sequence σ. Every set of P and of its reducts is one written in starts
#   or pick, or {}; each chosen branch is the whole term, every call being
#   in tail position. The terms β-equal to {} (a failed prefix test, the
#   end of pick's list, the end of the string, a suffix too short) reach no
#   pure term (Empty). So a pure term is reached only at a position j,
#   through a sequence of words, each a prefix of what the string has left
#   after the ones before, until every word is used once: the string from j
#   begins with the words concatenated in that order, and the term is
#   β-equal to ⌜j⌝.
#
# THE SUBTASKS AND THE TRAPS: concatenation_point/6 below. The string is
# random bits with some concatenations of the words, in random orders,
# spliced in, and some near misses (a concatenation with one word replaced
# by another word of the list, so one word twice and one missing). The trap
# takes the string of a subtask with the middle bit of each matching window
# flipped, until no window matches, its snapshot empty.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHM: at each position, the pieces of the width counted
# against the multiset of the words, the counts kept as the window slides
# by one width (the standard solution of LeetCode 30), the words found by
# string matching (D. E. Knuth, J. H. Morris, V. R. Pratt, Fast Pattern
# Matching in Strings, SIAM J. Comput. 1977, doi:10.1137/0206024); tabled
# evaluation keyed by the value of the position and of the words left (W.
# Chen, D. S. Warren, J. ACM 1996, doi:10.1145/227595.227597).

#   THE SUBTASKS: concatenation_point(?Point, ?Width, ?Words, ?Distinct,
#   ?Seed, ?Kind): Words words of Width bits drawn from Distinct distinct
#   words, so that many are equal; the string is made by point_string/3.
#   Sized by a probe of 2026-09-30 (CPUs 0-5, 1024 MB stack and
#   table, the machine shared), each reading equal to its snapshot:
#   tabling(weak_head) reads six words (91 bits, 5 positions) in 1454 ms
#   and ten (194 bits, 3 positions) in 6051 ms, and is stopped at 60 s on
